  about: [
    `<em>The Art of War</em> is the oldest and best-known military treatise to survive from ancient China. It runs to thirteen short chapters, each taking up one part of the business of war — calculation, supply, stratagem, formation, ground, fire, spies — and treating it in a few pages of compressed precepts. Tradition credits it to Sun Wu, Master Sun, a general said to have served the state of Wu in the late sixth century BCE. Whether he lived, or whether the text gathers up a school of military thought over several generations, is a question scholars have not settled.`,
    `The surprising thing about the book is that it argues against battle as the aim of strategy. Sun Tzu is no pacifist and takes war entirely seriously, but he ranks fighting low among a general's options. In Calthrop's words, "the supreme art is to subdue the enemy without fighting." A commander who must fight has already given up something a better commander would have kept. That idea explains why soldiers, statesmen and business readers have returned to the book for centuries. The edition in the Tinct reader is the English translation by Captain E. F. Calthrop, published by John Murray in 1908 as <em>The Book of War</em>, alongside a Tinct modern English rendering of it.`,
  ],
  chaptersSubtitle: 'All 13 chapters — from the preliminary reckoning to the employment of spies.',
  chaptersLead: `<p>The Art of War is built as thirteen self-contained teachings. The opening chapters set out the foundations: the reckoning made before a campaign, the cost of keeping troops on campaign, and the preference for stratagem over assault. The middle chapters move into operations — formation, the spirit of the troops, the use of emptiness and strength, marching and ground. The closing chapter, on spies, returns to the opening theme of knowledge and reads as a summary of the whole. The book can be read straight through in an afternoon or consulted one chapter at a time.</p>`,
